1
00:00:00,462 --> 00:00:01,610
Land in Kochi.

2
00:00:02,028 --> 00:00:03,130
Land in London.

3
00:00:03,631 --> 00:00:04,822
Land in Tokyo.

4
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:07,054
The runway numbers look the same.

5
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:09,454
The white piano keys look the same.

6
00:00:09,903 --> 00:00:12,374
Even the lights that guide you down look
the same.

7
00:00:12,726 --> 00:00:13,853
That is not luck.

8
00:00:14,870 --> 00:00:17,316
Somebody wrote one rulebook for the whole
sky.

9
00:00:17,697 --> 00:00:20,388
And in the next ten minutes, you'll know
exactly who.

10
00:00:20,888 --> 00:00:22,994
Air Regulations, episode one.

11
00:00:23,477 --> 00:00:25,205
Who writes the rules of the sky?

12
00:00:25,818 --> 00:00:26,881
Here's the plan.

13
00:00:27,285 --> 00:00:28,263
One treaty.

14
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:30,697
One United Nations agency.

15
00:00:31,166 --> 00:00:35,081
And one chain of law that runs all the way
down to your DGCA exam.

16
00:00:35,989 --> 00:00:36,992
Chapter one.

17
00:00:37,394 --> 00:00:38,930
How the sky got its rules.

18
00:00:39,782 --> 00:00:42,125
1903: the Wright brothers fly.

19
00:00:42,479 --> 00:00:47,329
Within sixteen years, aircraft are
crossing borders, so in 1919, the Paris

20
00:00:47,349 --> 00:00:50,140
Convention writes the first international
air law.

21
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:55,716
1929: the Warsaw Convention sets the rules
on airline liability.

22
00:00:56,110 --> 00:01:00,465
Then, in the middle of World War Two,
delegates from fifty-four nations meet in

23
00:01:00,485 --> 00:01:01,326
Chicago.

24
00:01:02,336 --> 00:01:07,552
Fifty-two of those States signed the
Convention on 7 December 1944.

25
00:01:08,467 --> 00:01:12,997
Its official name: the Convention on
International Civil Aviation, ninety-six

26
00:01:13,017 --> 00:01:13,857
articles.

27
00:01:14,318 --> 00:01:19,198
And the article every examiner loves is
Article One: every State has complete and

28
00:01:19,218 --> 00:01:22,773
exclusive sovereignty over the airspace
above its territory.

29
00:01:23,276 --> 00:01:26,771
It came into force on 4 April 1947.

30
00:01:27,827 --> 00:01:33,012
Quiz bonus: the signing date, 7 December,
is now celebrated every year as

31
00:01:33,032 --> 00:01:35,231
International Civil Aviation Day.

32
00:01:36,314 --> 00:01:38,358
So what counts as a State's territory?

33
00:01:38,821 --> 00:01:39,969
Start with the land.

34
00:01:40,405 --> 00:01:45,310
Add the territorial sea, for India, twelve
nautical miles out from the baseline along

35
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:46,133
the coast.

36
00:01:46,576 --> 00:01:48,919
All the airspace above both is sovereign.

37
00:01:49,159 --> 00:01:50,862
Beyond that lie the high seas.

38
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:56,055
Nobody owns that sky, and Article Twelve
says the ICAO Rules of the Air apply

39
00:01:56,075 --> 00:01:56,875
there.

40
00:01:57,689 --> 00:02:01,769
So sovereign means one simple thing: no
foreign aircraft enters without

41
00:02:01,789 --> 00:02:02,589
permission.

42
00:02:03,464 --> 00:02:04,488
Chapter two.

43
00:02:04,922 --> 00:02:06,711
ICAO, the referee of the sky.

44
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:12,019
The Convention also created an
organisation to run it: ICAO, the

45
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:14,775
International Civil Aviation Organization.

46
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:19,235
It's a specialised agency of the United
Nations, with its headquarters in

47
00:02:19,255 --> 00:02:20,727
Montreal, Canada.

48
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:26,145
And until enough States had ratified, a
provisional version, PICAO, did the job

49
00:02:26,165 --> 00:02:27,678
from 1945.

50
00:02:28,628 --> 00:02:30,267
Don't mix it up with IATA.

51
00:02:30,753 --> 00:02:32,542
ICAO is made of governments.

52
00:02:32,973 --> 00:02:35,632
IATA is the airlines' own trade
association.

53
00:02:36,694 --> 00:02:40,282
Today, ICAO has one hundred and
ninety-three member States.

54
00:02:40,687 --> 00:02:46,037
India ratified the Convention on
1 March 1947, a month before it

55
00:02:46,057 --> 00:02:47,594
even came into force.

56
00:02:48,672 --> 00:02:49,590
Did you know?

57
00:02:50,070 --> 00:02:55,025
ICAO works in six official languages:
Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian

58
00:02:55,045 --> 00:02:55,962
and Spanish.

59
00:02:56,997 --> 00:03:00,553
ICAO also runs seven regional offices
around the world.

60
00:03:01,009 --> 00:03:05,439
For India, the one that matters is the
Asia and Pacific office, in Bangkok.

61
00:03:06,228 --> 00:03:11,003
Inside ICAO: the Assembly, every member
State, meeting at least once every three

62
00:03:11,023 --> 00:03:11,823
years.

63
00:03:12,129 --> 00:03:14,959
It elects the Council, the permanent
governing body.

64
00:03:15,454 --> 00:03:19,384
The Council is advised by the Air
Navigation Commission, the technical

65
00:03:19,404 --> 00:03:21,377
experts who draft the Annexes.

66
00:03:21,875 --> 00:03:26,607
And the Secretariat, led by the Secretary
General, runs the day-to-day work.

67
00:03:27,788 --> 00:03:28,809
Exam trap!

68
00:03:29,292 --> 00:03:32,378
Most books still say the Council has
thirty-six members.

69
00:03:32,937 --> 00:03:36,098
Since 12 June 2026, it's forty.

70
00:03:36,542 --> 00:03:40,784
The four new seats are filled at a special
Assembly in Montreal in November.

71
00:03:41,837 --> 00:03:43,924
So what does ICAO actually produce?

72
00:03:44,522 --> 00:03:47,048
SARPs, Standards and Recommended
Practices.

73
00:03:47,535 --> 00:03:51,702
A Standard says "shall": States must
follow it, or tell ICAO they don't.

74
00:03:52,165 --> 00:03:56,169
A Recommended Practice says "should":
desirable, but not mandatory.

75
00:03:57,010 --> 00:03:58,585
And if a State can't comply?

76
00:03:59,076 --> 00:04:02,695
Article Thirty-Eight: it must notify ICAO
immediately.

77
00:04:03,045 --> 00:04:07,570
India publishes its differences in the
AIP, section GEN 1.7.

78
00:04:08,350 --> 00:04:10,480
Those Standards live in nineteen Annexes.

79
00:04:10,858 --> 00:04:15,183
You'll meet all of them in this series,
but start with four: Annex One, Personnel

80
00:04:15,203 --> 00:04:16,066
Licensing.

81
00:04:16,518 --> 00:04:18,157
Annex Two, Rules of the Air.

82
00:04:18,624 --> 00:04:20,715
Annex Six, Operation of Aircraft.

83
00:04:21,070 --> 00:04:23,400
Annex Eleven, Air Traffic Services.

84
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:26,025
And those identical runways from the
start?

85
00:04:26,591 --> 00:04:28,704
That's Annex Fourteen, Aerodromes.

86
00:04:29,820 --> 00:04:35,100
Annex Fourteen is why a pilot from Kochi
can land in London with no surprises: the

87
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:39,395
same holding lines, the same edge lights,
the same markings, everywhere on the

88
00:04:39,415 --> 00:04:40,215
planet.

89
00:04:41,374 --> 00:04:43,675
Here's a way to remember those four
Annexes.

90
00:04:44,127 --> 00:04:45,290
One: the pilot.

91
00:04:45,646 --> 00:04:46,645
Two: the rules.

92
00:04:47,147 --> 00:04:48,509
Six: the aeroplane.

93
00:04:48,885 --> 00:04:50,337
Eleven: the controller.

94
00:04:51,616 --> 00:04:56,721
Key point: under the Annexes sit the PANS,
Procedures for Air Navigation Services,

95
00:04:56,741 --> 00:04:58,169
the detailed how-to.

96
00:04:58,601 --> 00:05:01,349
PANS-ATM is Doc 4444.

97
00:05:01,776 --> 00:05:04,059
PANS-OPS is Doc 8168.

98
00:05:04,493 --> 00:05:06,601
These two numbers come up again and again.

99
00:05:07,959 --> 00:05:09,004
Chapter three.

100
00:05:09,482 --> 00:05:11,632
From Chicago to your DGCA exam.

101
00:05:12,383 --> 00:05:14,108
Here's the chain of law in India.

102
00:05:14,603 --> 00:05:17,415
At the top: the Chicago Convention and its
Annexes.

103
00:05:17,878 --> 00:05:23,119
Parliament turns them into law with the
Bharatiya Vayuyan Adhiniyam, 2024.

104
00:05:23,486 --> 00:05:26,128
The Central Government makes Rules under
that Act.

105
00:05:26,513 --> 00:05:30,007
DGCA issues the Civil Aviation
Requirements, the CARs.

106
00:05:30,404 --> 00:05:34,807
And the Airports Authority of India
publishes the AIP, AICs and NOTAMs.

107
00:05:35,741 --> 00:05:40,066
The Act itself: Act number sixteen of
2024, in force from

108
00:05:40,086 --> 00:05:42,671
1 January 2025.

109
00:05:43,070 --> 00:05:47,265
It replaced the Aircraft Act of 1934,
after ninety years.

110
00:05:47,811 --> 00:05:53,410
Section three sets up DGCA, section five
BCAS, section seven the AAIB,

111
00:05:53,430 --> 00:05:57,085
and section eleven lets the government
make rules to implement the Chicago

112
00:05:57,105 --> 00:05:57,988
Convention.

113
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:00,445
Exam trap number two.

114
00:06:00,870 --> 00:06:05,236
If the old Act is gone, are the Aircraft
Rules of 1937 dead?

115
00:06:05,720 --> 00:06:06,520
No!

116
00:06:06,854 --> 00:06:10,665
Section forty-three keeps them working
until the new rules are published.

117
00:06:11,098 --> 00:06:15,188
In October, the government told the
Supreme Court the new rules were approved

118
00:06:15,208 --> 00:06:18,439
and would be published within weeks, so
know both names.

119
00:06:19,453 --> 00:06:24,206
Who's who under the Ministry of Civil
Aviation: DGCA, the safety regulator,

120
00:06:24,226 --> 00:06:26,649
licences, airworthiness, the CARs.

121
00:06:27,045 --> 00:06:29,119
BCAS, aviation security.

122
00:06:29,532 --> 00:06:31,691
AAIB, accident investigation.

123
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:35,747
And AAI, which runs airports and air
navigation services.

124
00:06:36,969 --> 00:06:39,121
Studying for EASA or the UK CAA?

125
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:41,280
Same Chicago foundation.

126
00:06:41,733 --> 00:06:44,945
In Europe, EASA writes common rules for
its member States.

127
00:06:45,332 --> 00:06:49,308
In Britain, the UK CAA regulates under the
Air Navigation Order.

128
00:06:49,777 --> 00:06:52,927
So the ICAO part of this lesson is
identical for you.

129
00:06:53,994 --> 00:06:54,912
Quick check.

130
00:06:55,354 --> 00:06:59,259
Which Article gives every State complete
and exclusive sovereignty over its

131
00:06:59,279 --> 00:07:00,125
airspace?

132
00:07:00,606 --> 00:07:01,584
Think about it.

133
00:07:04,646 --> 00:07:05,687
Article One.

134
00:07:06,018 --> 00:07:09,043
Article Two only defines what the
territory is.

135
00:07:10,235 --> 00:07:11,874
Where is ICAO headquartered?

136
00:07:12,344 --> 00:07:13,510
Lock in your answer.

137
00:07:16,589 --> 00:07:20,231
Montreal, Canada, not Chicago, where the
Convention was signed.

138
00:07:21,410 --> 00:07:24,620
Last one: how many States sit on the ICAO
Council today?

139
00:07:25,154 --> 00:07:26,175
Three seconds.

140
00:07:29,281 --> 00:07:31,514
Forty, since June 2026.

141
00:07:32,656 --> 00:07:33,697
Let's recap.

142
00:07:34,229 --> 00:07:39,576
Chicago, 1944: ninety-six articles, in
force in 1947.

143
00:07:39,911 --> 00:07:41,635
Article One: sovereignty.

144
00:07:42,095 --> 00:07:47,210
ICAO: a UN agency in Montreal, one hundred
and ninety-three States, and a

145
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:48,533
Council of forty.

146
00:07:49,053 --> 00:07:52,015
Standards say shall; recommended practices
say should.

147
00:07:52,479 --> 00:07:56,609
And in India: the Act, the Rules, the
CARs, then the AIP and NOTAMs.

148
00:07:57,897 --> 00:08:02,663
Next episode: the Chicago Convention
articles DGCA actually asks,

149
00:08:02,683 --> 00:08:07,163
cabotage, the documents every aircraft
must carry, and the Article that trips

150
00:08:07,183 --> 00:08:08,080
everyone up.

151
00:08:08,594 --> 00:08:10,233
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

152
00:08:10,644 --> 00:08:13,456
A new lesson every day at ten fifteen in
the morning.
